# .workerignore -- STATUS: LIVE, 2026-11-09 (design: docs/design/V3_CI_TIERS_AND_IGNORE_LADDER.md section 4;
# evidence in traces/ignore_ladder_20261105/; the strip step: docs/CI_V3.md, "Stripped tree").
#
# Who reads it (CODE READ 2026-11-09):
#   1. The Vast box. scripts/bench/box/merge_check.sh runs `scripts/ci/ignore_ladder.py strip --in-place`
#      in its checkout of the tested commit: every tracked file that a line below matches is deleted
#      before any box stage runs, so a box test cannot depend on an ignored path. NOT YET RUN ON
#      HARDWARE: the first gpu-vast run on a batch PR is its verification.
#   2. The stripped-tree CI job (.github/workflows/ci.yml, job `stripped`): the same strip with the same
#      matcher, then the GPU-free Rust build, test and clippy. The commit's OWN copy of this file is
#      read (git show REV:.workerignore), never the working copy.
#   3. The CI Worker's result store (ci/vast-broker/src/treekey.js, PR #258): it reads this file from the
#      root of every commit it records, to compute the commit's tree key. The key is the hash of the
#      unignored files plus the effective lines of this file, so adding a line changes the key.
# ONE matcher: `matches` in scripts/ci/ignore_ladder.py. The Worker's JavaScript matcher is a second
# implementation and is cross-checked against it (ci/vast-broker/test/ladder-parity.test.js: the real
# files, 400 seeded adversarial pattern sets, the key bytes) and against `git check-ignore`.
#
# Fail closed (exit 3, nothing deleted): a missing file, a line outside the syntax below, an ignore set
# that would delete code under crates/ qemu/ cuda/ firmware/ tools/ .cargo/ or the two oracle
# submodules, Cargo.* / rust-toolchain.toml / clippy.toml / .gitmodules / .dockerignore / Dockerfile*
# (prose .md and .gitignore files excepted), or a tree with nothing left. A test that needs an ignored
# path is fixed by moving its data next to it or by listing the NEIGHBOURS here instead of the
# directory (the syntax has no negation); never by relaxing the test.
#
# OWNER DECISION NEEDED, nothing here is changed silently: the line `/ci/vast-broker` below ignores a
# code root that treekey.js PROTECTED refuses, so the Worker refuses this whole file and
# produces NO tree key today for any commit that carries it: every commit is stored under its
# per-commit key (tree_kind 'commit') and no result falls through between commits. TREE_KEY_CARRY_OVER stays off (unset). This change neither drops the line nor
# shrinks PROTECTED. What changed since the refusal was written (review of PR #269, R1: "the box does
# not remove ignored files, so a key would let a result answer for files the run may have read"): the
# box now deletes them. Two ways out:
#   (A) drop `/ci/vast-broker` here and in .dockerignore: the Worker accepts the file and keys by tree,
#       a Worker-only change moves the key (the gate runs; the Worker is part of what it validates),
#       and nothing else changes. RECOMMENDED until a stripped box has passed a real gate.
#   (B) keep the line and remove ci/vast-broker from PROTECTED: sound only if no box stage reads it
#       (scripts/ci/test_box_stripped.py proves the static half) AND the gate-skip list
#       (ci/vast-broker/trigger-policy.mjs, which never skips ci/vast-broker/) is changed the same way.
# Do not drop lines or shrink PROTECTED to obtain tree keys without that decision.
#
# The ladder:  .gitignore  is a subset of  .workerignore  is a subset of  .dockerignore.
# Line based and checked by scripts/ci/ignore_ladder.py. Every line of the group below is
# copied from .gitignore; the checker fails if a line is missing.
#
# A test result is keyed by the hash of the sorted UNIGNORED tracked files (path, mode, blob id), so a
# change confined to ignored paths shares the result of its parent.
#
# Syntax (asserted by the checker): every line starts with / or **/, no negation, no trailing /,
# `**` only as a whole segment. git, docker and the checker's matcher then read it the same way.

# --- traces/: ignored EXCEPT the evidence that tests read (include_str! and runtime reads) ---
# The syntax has no negation, so the kept directories are listed by being left out: each line below is the
# shortest prefix glob that matches no kept name. Kept (a test of a workspace crate reads them; the box
# sparse list in ci/vast-broker/src/onstart.js names the same data): driver_matrix, rawclient_ce_interrupt_20261008, blackwell_host_caps_review_20261022, blackwell_hw_20261103, blackwell_provider_validation_20261023, blackwell_windows_resume_20261023, real_ga106, c_oracle_census, user_ring_scan_20261014, constants_night_20261014, real_ad104, real_ga104, real_hw_20261013, real_tu116, die_driver_sweep_20261015, constants_measure_20261014b, cap1_coldboot_hermetic.rec, cap1b_coldboot_hermetic_d6.rec.
# A new traces/ directory whose name starts like one of these globs is ignored without a line; one that
# starts like a kept name stays in the stripped tree (harmless, it only enters the key).
# ORDER MATTERS TO THE WORKER, NOT TO THE STRIP: treekey.js stops at the first line that matches, and it
# must list traces/ (part of it is kept, so it cannot skip the directory), so these lines come FIRST and
# the broadest first. Measured 2026-11-09: 7.5 M of the Worker matcher's 20 M steps in this order,
# more than 20 M (no key) when they follow the other lines. ci/vast-broker/test/ladder-parity.test.js
# fails above 60 percent of the budget.
/traces/w*
/traces/v*
/traces/die_c*
/traces/s*
/traces/g*
/traces/a*
/traces/bo*
/traces/t*
/traces/p*
/traces/blackwell_v*
/traces/blackwell_r*
/traces/l*
/traces/blackwell_win_*
/traces/blackwell_c*
/traces/rec*
/traces/blackwell_a*
/traces/capa*
/traces/blackwell_f*
/traces/blackwell_e*
/traces/real_w*
/traces/o*
/traces/dis*
/traces/blackwell_n*
/traces/die_s*
/traces/caps*
/traces/k*
/traces/can*
/traces/i*
/traces/rel*
/traces/f*
/traces/cw*
/traces/do*
/traces/m*
/traces/de*
/traces/h*
/traces/e*
/traces/n*
/traces/cu*
/traces/rawclient_a*
/traces/rp*
/traces/ru*
/traces/E*
/traces/bw*
/traces/2*
